--TEST--
Core\IO::temporaryDir is refused with no configuration at all, like every other member of the class, and the refusal is a catchable RuntimeError rather than a fatal — so a program that only wanted somewhere to work can degrade instead of dying — `rule:security/capability-question-is-grant-and-scope` and `rule:security/denial-is-a-runtime-error`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// Deny by default, on the member that takes no argument at all: there is no
// path in the call for a grant to be compared against, and the answer is still
// no. The member with nothing to check is exactly the one an exemption would
// have looked reasonable on.
int $refusals = 0;

try {
    string $made = Core\IO::temporaryDir();
    echo "temporaryDir answered\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $refused) {
    $refusals = $refusals + 1;
}

// Asked twice, because a door that cached its decision would answer once and
// then let the second call through.
try {
    string $again = Core\IO::temporaryDir();
    echo "temporaryDir answered on the second call\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $refused) {
    $refusals = $refusals + 1;
}

echo "refusals: ", $refusals as string, "\n";

// § 5's other half: a denial is known before any work is done, so the program
// is still running and still has its own state. A fatal would have printed
// nothing after this point.
echo "still running\n";
--EXPECT--
refusals: 2
still running
